<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sternpost-rudder">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sternpost rudder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Sternpost-mounted rudder, Stern-mounted rudder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sternpost rudder is a steering blade hung at the centre of a ship's stern, first known from pottery models of Chinese junks of the 1st century CE, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Unlike the steering oars used earlier, it did not get in the way of the sails, needed less effort from the helmsman and suited larger ocean-going ships. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the oldest known depiction of a pintle-and-gudgeon rudder dates from around 1180, and Joseph Needham held that the stern-mounted rudder reached Europe and the Islamic world from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sternpost-rudder.jpg" alt="Sternpost rudder" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Lee Todd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Han_pottery_boat.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1st century CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Guangzhou, China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some count the steering oars of Egypt and Rome as rudders; Joseph Needham held that the sternpost rudder reached Europe from China.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sail">Sail<span>6th millennium BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">Caravel<span>c. 1255</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=sternpost-rudder">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
